    <statement><p>Find <m>x</m> and <m>y</m> such that <m>5, x, y, 44</m> is part of an arithmetic sequence.</p>
<p><m>x =</m> <fillin characters="5" name="AnSwEr0001"/>, <m>y =</m> <fillin characters="5" name="AnSwEr0002"/></p>
<p>Then find <m>x</m> and <m>y</m> so that the sequence is part of a geometric sequence.</p>
<p><m>x =</m> <fillin characters="5" name="AnSwEr0003"/>, <m>y =</m> <fillin characters="5" name="AnSwEr0004"/></p>
<p>(Warning: <m>x</m> and <m>y</m> might not be integers.)</p>
</statement>
    <answer>
      <p>
        <m>18</m>
      </p>
    </answer>
    <answer>
      <p>
        <m>31</m>
      </p>
    </answer>
    <answer>
      <p>
        <m>5\cdot 8.8^{\frac{1}{3}}</m>
      </p>
    </answer>
    <answer>
      <p>
        <m>5\cdot 8.8^{\frac{2}{3}}</m>
      </p>
    </answer>
    <solution><p>For arithmetic: <m>x = 18 \text{,}</m> <m>y = 31 \text{.}</m> We know that <m>5 + d = x\text{,}</m> <m>x + d = y\text{,}</m> and <m>y + d = 44\text{.}</m>  In other words, <m>5 + 3d = 44</m> so <m>d = (39)/3\text{.}</m></p>
<p>For geometric: <m>x = {10.3228}</m> and <m>y = {21.312}\text{.}</m>    We can find the common ratio for the geometric sequence by solving <m>5\cdot r^3 = 44</m> for <m>r\text{.}</m></p>
</solution>
